# Public site image weight (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-10-30): compress and stop re-downloading
large analoginfo.com files. Webflow Technical Support (Marcel) reported
that `cmmc-chaos-to-order.png` was about 1 GB across about 590 requests
in the reviewed period. Every request was served from cache and still
counted. All of it was automated: missing User-Agent plus headless
Chrome referred from our own site. The file was 1.72 MB with no
responsive variants, so each request downloaded the whole thing.
Several other blog PNGs were between 1.8 MB and 3.4 MB. The October 19
peak included Python pulling those PNGs and competitor PDFs.

ChatGPT already uploaded optimized blog covers for the twenty published
articles in `bandwidth-20261030-r1` and bound heroes / cards to the
CMS **Optimized cover image** field (500 KB maximum). Do not replay
that release. Do not re-upload those covers. Fifteen draft items still
need an approved optimized cover before any future authorized publish.

## The rule

> **Every analoginfo.com delivery image is sized for the slot it
> fills, has server-rendered `srcset` and `sizes` in the initial
> HTML, and has a reasonably sized fallback. Routine checks are HEAD.
> A full body download is allowed only for an explicitly changed
> asset that has no verified local copy, once, then retained. No bulk
> PNG, font, video, PDF, or competitor-document pulls.**

## Delivery ceilings (authoring)

| Slot | Target encoded size | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Thumbnail / card | 150 KB | Document a readability or lossless exception before release |
| Article hero / cover | 500 KB | Matches the CMS Optimized cover image maximum |
| Single integrity download | 4 MB | Hard stop; investigate instead of raising |
| Changed images per run | 10 explicit URLs | Share the HTML run/day budget |

Never silently crush legibility to hit a number. If the artwork cannot
meet the target without becoming unreadable, record the exception and
keep the approved art.

## Responsive variants

Initial HTML must include `srcset` and `sizes`. JavaScript-only swaps
do not count. Webflow CSV image imports do **not** create native
responsive variants; upload delivery images directly in the Designer
or provide explicit server-rendered sources.

Prefer WebP / AVIF for on-page delivery where the template supports
them. Social sharing and Open Graph images stay a supported PNG or
JPEG. Do not substitute WebP / AVIF for Open Graph. Verify the actual
file format; do not trust the filename (a JPEG served as `.png` has
already occurred).

Preserve approved artwork and alt text
([`public-site-index-and-image-tags.mdc`](public-site-index-and-image-tags.mdc)).

## What to request on the wire

| Check | Method |
| --- | --- |
| Availability / size of an unchanged image | HEAD, once per unique URL per run |
| Metadata, canonical, JSON-LD | HTML only; no image body |
| New or changed artwork integrity | Local file hash and decode first; GET the remote body only if no verified local copy exists |
| Unchanged PDF / Word | Reuse the verified local file |
| Competitor or comparison PDF already on disk | Do not GET it again from analoginfo.com |

`python bandwidth_assets.py <changed-image-URL> ...` is the integrity
path. It shares the run/day budget in
[`public-site-bandwidth-budget.mdc`](public-site-bandwidth-budget.mdc).
Unsupported HEAD, a bad status, or a bad content type is a failure to
investigate. Never fall back to downloading the image.

## Worked violation (do not repeat)

**Violation.** A validator or ChatGPT / Cursor session GETs
`cmmc-chaos-to-order.png` (1.72 MB, no `srcset`) hundreds of times.
CDN cache hits. Webflow still bills. About 1 GB for one file.

**Fix.** Author a 1080-pixel delivery derivative under 500 KB with
native responsive variants. HEAD for routine checks. Keep the local
file. Do not bulk-GET the original PNG or the comparison PDFs.

## Forbidden

- Shipping a blog cover or hero above 500 KB without a recorded
  exception
- Serving one 1.7 MB-class PNG to every card and every viewport
- Webflow CSV as the path to "add responsive images"
- Bulk GET of blog PNGs, fonts, video, or competitor PDFs
- A `--full-images` whole-blog body audit
- Re-uploading the October 30 optimized covers
- Publishing any of the fifteen draft blog items without an approved
  optimized cover
- Disabling images in the browser to fake a low transfer count

## Required agent behavior

1. Author and cache the delivery file locally before any live GET.
2. Confirm `srcset` / `sizes` in the initial production HTML for
   changed templates, using one bounded page GET or a cached copy.
3. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same
   change set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).

## Origin

2026-10-30 - Webflow Support: one 1.72 MB blog PNG cost about 1 GB
across about 590 automated cached requests; other blog PNGs 1.8-3.4 MB;
October 19 Python pulled those PNGs and competitor PDFs. Strategy
change: weight the file, require `srcset`, and stop bulk downloads.
